      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Calcific deposits located within the tendons of the rotator cuff are frequently seen in patients presenting with shoulder pain. The pathogenesis of calcific tendinitis and the optimum management of patients presenting with acute symptoms are unclear. This paper reviews the incidence, proposed etiologies, and a unique treatment approach of rotator cuff calcific tendinitis. A case report of a patient with acute calcific tendinitis and subsequent shoulder motion and strength deficits is presented. A rational evaluation and treatment plan is outlined, which includes management and posttreatment changes, and radiographic findings are discussed. A team-management approach by physical therapy and orthopaedics services is emphasized."},
